<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("max_steps") %>
<% budgets = sweep.fetch(:param_grid).fetch("max_steps") %>
<% seeds = sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %>
<% planned = budgets.size * seeds %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% default_budget = Lab::Schema.run_defaults.fetch("max_steps") %>
<% rate_exponent = -Math.log2(Lab::EMERGENT_MUTATION_RATE).round %>
<% terminal = findings_finished_count(experiment_slug: "max-steps") %>

<%# The recorded result, read on 2026-09-11. Runs are named by arm and seed, never by a
    database id, and the live tables further down re-read the same runs. %>
<% arms = [
     { steps: 256, exponent: 8, flagged: 0, confirmed: 0, transitions: [], rate: 74.35, blips: 1 },
     { steps: 1_024, exponent: 10, flagged: 0, confirmed: 0, transitions: [], rate: 62.60, blips: 2 },
     { steps: 8_192, exponent: 13, flagged: 2, confirmed: 2, transitions: [[1, 5_030, 867], [5, 7_000, 108]],
       rate: 29.49, blips: 4 },
     { steps: 65_536, exponent: 16, flagged: 0, confirmed: 0, transitions: [], rate: 5.96, blips: 5 }
   ] %>

<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 4 varies the only parameter that is purely about computation.
  <span class="mono">max_steps</span> is how many instructions one interaction may execute
  before the pair of tapes is put back in the world, and the reading it was queued for is a
  floor: a copy loop needs a certain number of instructions to run to the end, so a budget
  below that length cannot finish a copy however lucky the tape is, and emergence should be
  impossible under it. Above the floor the budget is supposed to stop mattering — a
  replicator that fits in 8 000 steps also fits in 60 000, and the extra head-room is spent
  on tapes that were going to halt anyway. The prediction is therefore monotone and then
  flat: nothing, nothing, something, the same something.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  One parameter moving over four budgets two octaves apart —
  <%= safe_join(arms.map { |arm| tag.span(safe_join(["2", tag.sup(arm.fetch(:exponent))]), class: "mono") },
                ", ") %>
  instructions, that is
  <span class="mono"><%= budgets.map { |steps| number_with_delimiter(steps, delimiter: " ") }.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span>
  — on a 128×128 torus of 64-byte tapes, <%= seeds %> seeds per arm and <%= epochs %>
  epochs per run. The mutation rate is held at
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-<%= rate_exponent %></sup></span>, the rate of the first run of
  the programme to show emergence, so an arm that shows nothing cannot be explained by
  sitting outside the region where anything ever happened; every other parameter is at its
  default. The dependent variable is the transition epoch of DESIGN §1.2 — the first
  sampled epoch at which <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> falls below 0.6 and stays
  below for three further samples — and beside it the
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> census. A run that never crosses is censored at
  <%= epochs %> epochs, not counted as a zero.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  <%= terminal %> of the <%= planned %> runs have reached <%= epochs %> epochs — a count read
  from the database when this page is served rather than frozen into the text. The table below
  is the per-arm result recorded once every one of the <%= planned %> had finished; the phase
  diagram and the per-run rows further down read the same runs live.
</p>

<div class="table-scroll">
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th><span class="mono">max_steps</span></th>
        <th class="numeric">Flagged</th>
        <th class="numeric">Census-confirmed</th>
        <th>Transition epochs (peak census)</th>
        <th class="numeric">Disagreements</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% arms.each do |arm| %>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono"><%= number_with_delimiter arm.fetch(:steps), delimiter: " " %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:flagged) %> of <%= seeds %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:confirmed) %> of <%= seeds %></td>
          <td class="mono">
            <% if arm.fetch(:transitions).empty? %>
              —
            <% else %>
              <%= safe_join(arm.fetch(:transitions).sort_by { |transition| transition[1] }
                               .map { |seed, epoch, peak|
                    safe_join([number_with_delimiter(epoch, delimiter: " "), " (",
                               findings_run_link(experiment_slug: "max-steps", seed: seed,
                                                 params: { "max_steps" => arm.fetch(:steps) }),
                               ", #{peak})"])
                  }, ", ") %>
            <% end %>
          </td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:flagged) - arm.fetch(:confirmed) %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-muted">
  "Peak census" is the largest <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> the run reached at
  any sample. The last column is the runs the two observables disagree about — flagged by
  the detector with a census that never leaves zero, or counted by the census without the
  detector firing.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>More compute per interaction is not more life.</strong> Emergence appears at one
  budget only, <span class="mono"><%= number_with_delimiter default_budget, delimiter: " " %></span>
  steps, in 2 of <%= seeds %> seeds, and the arm eight times larger than it produces
  nothing. That is not the shape the hypothesis predicts. A floor would leave every arm
  above it emerging at about the same rate; what the sweep shows is 0, 0, 2 and 0 — a single
  positive point with silence on both sides of it. Read at face value the interaction budget
  has an optimum rather than a threshold, and spending more computation on each interaction
  is at best wasted and at worst harmful.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The detector and the census agree everywhere.</strong> Every arm's disagreement
  column is zero: the two runs the detector flags are exactly the two runs that count a
  replicating cell, and no run in the sweep does one without the other. That is worth
  saying because it is not what the rest of the programme looks like — the
  <%= link_to "mutation-rate sweep", finding_path("mutation-rate-window") %> and its
  <%= link_to "long re-run", finding_path("mutation-rate-long-horizon") %> both report runs
  where <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> collapses with an empty census. Here the
  cheap observable and the expensive one name the same two events, so nothing in this
  finding turns on which of them is believed.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The two transitions.</strong> The <%= number_with_delimiter default_budget, delimiter: " " %>
  arm's first seed crosses at epoch 5 030 and its census peaks at 867 replicating cells at
  5 080 — the largest count of any 128×128 run in the lab — having counted its first cell at
  4 890. Its entropy bottoms out at 4.85 bits at 5 120, its
  <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> peaks at 0.0474 at 5 090, and it ends the run
  collapsed: <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> 0.26, 13 349 distinct tapes,
  <span class="mono">top_share</span> 0.00427. The same arm's fifth seed crosses at 7 000,
  counts its first cell at 7 030 and climbs much more slowly — a census peak of 108 at
  9 580, a <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> peak of 0.00433 at 9 610, entropy down to
  4.32 bits at 7 100 — and ends the run far less consumed than the first:
  <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> 0.054, 2 406 distinct tapes,
  <span class="mono">top_share</span> 0.00726, a world taken over by one short lineage rather
  than by the 13 349 variants the first seed ends with. Both transitions are backed by stored
  worlds: the second has a snapshot at
  exactly its transition epoch, and the first has one 30 epochs before it, which is close
  enough to rescore the crossing against a rebuilt world rather than only against its
  samples.
</p>

<% cited_chart = findings_run_chart(experiment_slug: "max-steps", seed: 1, metric: "replicator_count",
                                    params: { "max_steps" => default_budget }) %>
<% if cited_chart %>
  <p>
    The first seed's census, with its transition epoch marked: 4 890 epochs of flat zero,
    then the whole event inside 200 epochs.
  </p>

  <%= cited_chart %>
<% end %>

<p>
  <strong>The silent arms are silent in every observable.</strong> Among the 38 runs that
  never transitioned, <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> never falls below 7.77 against
  a random-soup baseline near 7.94, the final <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> spans
  0.921 to 0.984, and <span class="mono">distinct_tapes</span> ends between 16 379 and
  16 384 of the world's 16 384 cells. That is an untouched soup, not a world that started
  down the path and stopped: the 256 and 1 024 arms do not sit part-way between the silent
  and the collapsed, they sit where the first epoch left them. <%= arms.sum { |arm| arm.fetch(:blips) } %>
  of those 38 runs do show a <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> blip, eleven of them at
  exactly 6.1 × 10<sup>-5</sup> — that is 1/16 384, one interaction of a counted epoch that
  copied a tape — and the twelfth at twice it. None of the twelve produces a replicator, and
  every one of them ends as untouched as the rest, so a blip is not an early sign of
  emergence. It is not noise spread evenly over the arms either: the count climbs with the
  budget, 1 of the 10 runs at 256, 2 of 10 at 1 024, 4 of the 8 silent runs at 8 192 and 5 of
  10 at 65 536. That is the first sign that the extra instructions are being executed and not
  merely allowed; the cost measured below says the same thing outright.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>What four points and two events can and cannot support.</strong> Each silent arm
  rests on <%= seeds %> seeds, and 0 of <%= seeds %> bounds a per-arm rate to roughly 0 to
  26% — the one-sided 95% Clopper–Pearson upper bound is 0.259 — so no arm here is a
  demonstrated zero, and in particular the 65 536 arm is not shown to be worse than the
  8 192 one: 2 of <%= seeds %> against 0 of <%= seeds %> is a one-sided Fisher exact
  <span class="mono">p = 0.24</span>. The non-monotonicity is the claim the data actually
  makes and it is the claim with the least support: it rests on four points two octaves
  apart with two events at the only positive one, and a peak located by a single arm is a
  peak located to within a factor of 64. What the sweep does establish is negative and
  useful — a budget of 256 or 1 024 steps is not obviously fatal in the way the floor
  reading expects, and a budget of 65 536 does not buy the emergence that the extra
  computation was supposed to make easier.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>A determinism cross-check, and a caveat with it.</strong> The
  <%= number_with_delimiter default_budget, delimiter: " " %> arm is the engine's default
  budget, so its ten runs repeat the parameters of the
  <%= link_to "mutation-rate", finding_path("mutation-rate-window") %> sweep's
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-<%= rate_exponent %></sup></span> arm and of the
  <%= link_to "long re-run", finding_path("mutation-rate-long-horizon") %>'s. Its two
  transitions are those sweeps' two earliest, at the same epochs and the same census peaks,
  5 030 with 867 cells and 7 000 with 108, from the same
  <span class="mono">(params, seed)</span> — the guarantee DESIGN §1.1 makes, checked rather
  than asserted, for the third time in the programme. The caveat is the other side of the
  same coin: that arm is not an independent sample, so this sweep holds 30 runs of new
  evidence and 10 of a repeat, and the one arm where emergence happens is the one arm whose
  events were already counted elsewhere.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>What it cost, and what the cost says.</strong> A run writes its samples in batches
  as it goes, so the epochs between two writes over the seconds between them measure the
  engine actually running. Dropping every gap longer than 300 seconds removes the time a run
  spent queued or waiting for a slot and leaves the time it spent working; what is left covers
  18 000 to 19 900 of each run's <%= epochs %> epochs. These are throughput figures under a
  shared load of about a dozen runs on a 16-core box, not single-run benchmarks — the mean
  number of runs in flight was 12.3, 12.4, 9.5 and 11.5 from the narrowest arm to the widest,
  which if anything flatters the expensive arms.
</p>

<div class="table-scroll">
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th><span class="mono">max_steps</span></th>
        <th class="numeric">Epochs per second</th>
        <th class="numeric">Milliseconds per epoch</th>
        <th class="numeric">Runs with a copy-rate blip</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% arms.each do |arm| %>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono"><%= number_with_delimiter arm.fetch(:steps), delimiter: " " %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_precision arm.fetch(:rate), precision: 1 %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_precision 1_000.0 / arm.fetch(:rate), precision: 1 %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono">
            <%= arm.fetch(:blips) %> of <%= seeds - arm.fetch(:flagged) %>
          </td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-muted">
  Epochs per second is the median of the arm's silent runs; the blip column counts the silent
  runs whose <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> ever left zero.
</p>

<p>
  An epoch at <%= number_with_delimiter arms.last.fetch(:steps), delimiter: " " %> steps costs
  about <%= (arms.first.fetch(:rate) / arms.last.fetch(:rate)).round %> times what an epoch at
  <%= arms.first.fetch(:steps) %> costs, and the price is close to linear in the ceiling: each
  extra step of budget adds
  <%= arms.each_cons(2).map { |low, high|
        number_with_precision(((1.0 / high.fetch(:rate) - 1.0 / low.fetch(:rate)) /
                               (high.fetch(:steps) - low.fetch(:steps))) * 1e6, precision: 1)
      }.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %>
  microseconds per epoch across the three gaps — a marginal price that barely moves over three
  octaves of budget. That is the shape you get when interactions are still running when the
  ceiling cuts them off. If most of them halted long before
  <span class="mono">max_steps</span>, raising the ceiling would cost almost nothing and the
  marginal price would fall away as the ceiling rose; it does not. The eight-fold budget
  difference between the widest arm and the only one that works is real work, done and
  charged for, and it buys nothing.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>And the transitions are the expensive runs.</strong> The two runs that crossed are
  the two slowest in their arm, and each slowed at its own crossing: the first seed ran at
  31.0 epochs per second before epoch 5 030 and 14.7 after it, the fifth at 32.5 before 7 000
  and 2.6 after. Neither slowdown is contention — both runs had fewer neighbours on the box
  after their transition than before, 9.2 and 8.6 against 12.5 and 11.0. A collapsed world is
  full of tapes that copy themselves and a copy loop is a long program, so the same binary on
  the same world executes far more instructions per epoch once there is something worth
  executing. The budget is spent where a program exists to spend it, and the silent arms spend
  it on tapes that loop until the ceiling stops them.
</p>

<p>
  This stays partial rather than published, and not because a run is outstanding — every arm
  is censored at <%= epochs %> epochs, where the
  <%= link_to "long re-run", finding_path("mutation-rate-long-horizon") %> found that half
  the emergence in its region of the parameter space happens later, so a silent arm here is a
  silent arm at this budget of epochs and not at any budget. And a silence of ours reads as a
  fact about the world only once the
  <%= link_to "BFF positive control", finding_path("bff-control") %> resolves its own census;
  until then three of these four arms say as much about the instrument.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  The obvious follow-up is a finer grid around the one positive point — 2 048, 4 096, 8 192,
  16 384 and 32 768 at 20 seeds each — which would say whether the peak is real and roughly
  where it sits, and 20 seeds would make a 2-of-10 against 0-of-10 difference resolvable
  rather than merely visible. That is a proposal and not a commitment: it is around 400 000
  run-epochs per arm, and the <%= link_to "world-size sweep", finding_path("world-size-scaling") %>
  reads emergence as a hazard per cell-epoch, which makes a wider world the cheaper place to
  buy events than a finer grid on a parameter that may turn out to be flat. What the
  non-monotonicity does argue for is keeping the whole grid rather than fixing
  <span class="mono">max_steps</span> at its default and never looking at it again.
</p>
